    {
      "filename": "Control dam for Whatcom Creek (40270741932).jpg",
      "title": "File:Control dam for Whatcom Creek (40270741932).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Control_dam_for_Whatcom_Creek_%2840270741932%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Control_dam_for_Whatcom_Creek_(40270741932).jpg",
      "width": 2304,
      "height": 1728,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Robert Ashworth from Bellingham, WA., USA",
      "description": "A small dam, which is mostly just the gates, controls the flow of water from Lake Whatcom into Whatcom Creek.  This basically keeps everyone happy.  Lake Whatcom doesn't get too low, in the dryer summer months so people who live around the lake can still use their boat docks and water intake pipes.  In the winter, extra water is let down the creek so the lake doesn't get too high.  Back in the 1980s, regulating the lake worked okay, but when too much extra water was sent down the creek, it flooded businesses along Iowa Street.  It was a classic case of the diverging interests of citizens in different areas.  Since then, better dikes have been built along the creek and the greenway area serves as a harmless place to put the water.  Hurray for greenways and trails.",
      "dateCreated": "2017-09-17 03:31",
      "categories": [
        "CC-BY-2.0",
        "Files uploaded by Artix Kreiger",
        "Flickr images reviewed by FlickreviewR 2",
        "Images by Robert Ashworth"
      ],
